---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PracticeNotes from '../../components/PracticeNotes.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import SeoFaq from '../../components/SeoFaq.astro';

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Project leadership for complex digital delivery',
  description: 'Outcome-led project leadership for complex digital work: verified state, dependencies, parallel delivery, validation and recovery.',
  provider: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: 'Kévin Barré',
    url: 'https://kbarre.fr',
  },
  areaServed: ['Nantes', 'France', 'Remote'],
  serviceType: ['Digital project management', 'Project leadership', 'Delivery coordination', 'Business and technical alignment'],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Project leadership for complex digital delivery"
  description="Outcome-led project leadership for complex digital work: make the real state visible, coordinate dependencies, secure validation and resume without starting over."
  structuredData={serviceSchema}
  locale="en"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Project leadership"
    title="Lead what is true. Not what is assumed."
    text="When a project spans several teams, suppliers, technical components or parallel workstreams, the main risk is not lack of activity. It is losing the difference between what is planned, delivered, verified, blocked or still uncertain."
    aside="My role: maintain a project state reliable enough to decide, coordinate, recover and deliver without turning reporting into comforting fiction."
    ctaHref="/en/contact/?request=project"
    ctaLabel="Discuss a complex project"
    visual="/images/heroes/project-management.png"
    visualAlt="Project board with backlog, priorities and work in progress"
    visualWidth={1536}
    visualHeight={1024}
    motif="/images/kit/waves.svg"
  />

  <section class="section section--night">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading
        eyebrow="The real problem"
        title="A project can be very busy without actually moving forward."
        text="The more people and workstreams involved, the easier it becomes to confuse activity, progress and validation."
        align="split"
      />
      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">01</span>
          <h3>What is reported</h3>
          <p>A task can be marked complete while a dependency is missing, an assumption remains untested or no acceptance has happened yet.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">02</span>
          <h3>What is actually usable</h3>
          <p>I keep delivery separate from evidence: reviewed, tested, accepted, still ambiguous or dependent on a decision.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">03</span>
          <h3>What supports a decision</h3>
          <p>A useful project view does more than display statuses. It shows enough context to know what can start, what must wait and what needs arbitration.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial-copy editorial-copy--night">
        <p>The goal is not to add controls for their own sake. It is to stop important decisions being made from optimistic, stale or incomplete information. That discipline matters most when projects change quickly, workstreams move in parallel and restarting later would otherwise require rebuilding the entire context.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--muted" id="verified-state">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading
        eyebrow="Verified-state leadership"
        title="Four outcomes I optimise for."
        text="The working method can change. The quality of the state used for decisions should not."
        align="split"
      />
      <div class="service-grid service-grid--four">
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">01</span>
          <h3>Turn ambiguity into an actionable path</h3>
          <p>Objectives, constraints, unknowns, priorities, dependencies and success criteria become explicit enough for people to act.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">02</span>
          <h3>Run parallel work without losing direction</h3>
          <p>Parallel delivery is useful only when ownership, dependencies and convergence points remain visible.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">03</span>
          <h3>Separate progress from proof</h3>
          <p>I keep delivered, validated, blocked, ambiguous and still-to-decide work distinct. A status does not replace verification.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">04</span>
          <h3>Make the project resumable</h3>
          <p>At any point, it should be possible to understand what exists, what is still reliable, what needs confirmation and the next useful action.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading
        eyebrow="What changes in practice"
        title="Less implicit context. More usable decisions."
        text="Robust project leadership should not depend on one person keeping everything in their head. It makes the important parts readable enough for the work to continue."
        align="split"
      />
      <ol class="process-list">
        <li><span>01</span><strong>Decide from a current state</strong><p>Assumptions, validation, blockers and open decisions stay visible instead of being flattened into one progress percentage.</p></li>
        <li><span>02</span><strong>See dependencies before they block</strong><p>Parallel work is connected to prerequisites and convergence points so conflicts are found before the end of the cycle.</p></li>
        <li><span>03</span><strong>Transfer without losing the reasoning</strong><p>Important decisions retain context: why they were made, what they enable and what they still do not prove.</p></li>
        <li><span>04</span><strong>Resume without re-auditing everything</strong><p>After a pause or ownership change, work resumes from an explicit state and a next action instead of an approximate reconstruction of history.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--night">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading
        eyebrow="When this approach becomes useful"
        title="Projects that need memory, coordination and evidence."
        text="It adds the most value when delivery is more complex than a simple sequence of tasks."
        align="split"
      />
      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">A</span>
          <h3>An existing project</h3>
          <p>There are already deliverables, decisions and context debt. Before restarting, we need to separate what is still valid from what must be revalidated.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">B</span>
          <h3>Several teams or suppliers</h3>
          <p>The main risk becomes desynchronisation: everyone moves, but dependencies, interfaces or validation criteria stop converging.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="service-card">
          <span class="service-card__number">C</span>
          <h3>A project with high uncertainty</h3>
          <p>When decisions still depend on tests, arbitration or missing information, uncertainty should remain visible rather than being converted prematurely into certainty.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--muted">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading
        eyebrow="Two leadership rules"
        title="Do not hide what is missing."
        text="A useful project view also shows what has not yet been proven."
        align="split"
      />
      <div class="service-scope">
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Done ≠ validated</p>
          <h3>Delivery and proof are two different states.</h3>
          <p>A feature can exist without being reviewed, tested in the right environment or accepted by the people who need it. Keeping those states distinct prevents the next decision being built on imaginary validation.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">Unknown ≠ hidden problem</p>
          <h3>An explicit uncertainty can be managed.</h3>
          <p>Missing information, an open decision or an untested assumption is not a weakness as long as it is visible, owned and connected to the next decision.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionHeading
        eyebrow="A hybrid background"
        title="Understand the technical constraints without losing the business need."
        text="A developer background followed by project-management roles lets me read technical constraints, translate them and challenge their impact without turning leadership into a purely technical conversation."
        align="split"
      />
      <div class="profile-intro__meta">
        <div><span>Digital practice</span><strong>15+ years</strong></div>
        <div><span>Web & digital project management</span><strong>2017 — 2022</strong></div>
        <div><span>Freelance activity</span><strong>Since 2014</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial-copy">
        <p>The role is not to have every answer. It is to know where the decision sits, what information is missing, who can produce it and when it becomes reliable enough to commit the next step.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/en/digital-project-management/">See the project-management offer <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/en/agent-orchestration/">See the multi-agent orchestration approach <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeoFaq
    eyebrow="Common questions"
    title="About complex project leadership."
    intro="This approach is mainly for contexts where dependencies, validation and continuity matter as much as execution."
    items={[
      {
        question: 'Is this a proprietary project-management method?',
        answer: 'No. I use conventional practices such as scoping, prioritisation, dependency management, acceptance and documentation. The distinction is the discipline: do not present an assumption as a fact, a status as proof or a recovery as a fresh start.',
      },
      {
        question: 'Does the team need to replace its existing tools?',
        answer: 'Not necessarily. Jira, GitHub Projects, Trello, Asana, Notion or a shared document can all work. The tool matters less than the quality of the state represented inside it.',
      },
      {
        question: 'Can this help a project that is already struggling?',
        answer: 'Yes. A first phase can reconstruct the verified state: what exists, what remains reliable, open decisions, dependencies and the next action. The aim is to preserve what works rather than automatically starting over.',
      },
      {
        question: 'Does this work with distributed teams or several suppliers?',
        answer: 'Yes. Those are exactly the contexts where recorded decisions, explicit dependencies and shared validation points reduce desynchronisation risk.',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section section--purple">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="callout">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Complex project, recovery or cross-functional coordination</p>
          <h2>Start by rebuilding a state we can actually decide from.</h2>
        </div>
        <a class="button button--light" href="/en/contact/?request=project">Discuss the project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <PracticeNotes focus="project" locale="en" />
</BaseLayout>
